In article <4js7ue$r7i@saturn.haverford.edu>,
   brian@ada.brynmawr.edu (Doherty Brian J) wrote:
>
>
>I'm trying to use my OS/2 NFS client software to mount a directory 
>from  OSF/1 (v 3.2C, on an AlphaServer 1000) and am having problems.
>
>RPC.PCNFSD is running on the OSF/1 machine, and the appropriate
>directory (with my machine name) is in the exports file - but I can 
>not mount it successfully.
>
>I get through the PCNFSD-style login procedure and then I get a report
>of an unknown error (rc:7) and also a report of:
>
>RPC_AUTH_ERROR; why - AUTH_TOO_WEAK (remote error)

I am using OS/2 as well, and it works! Two things that you have to look out 
for: every time you do a change in the /etc/exports on the Digital Unix 
machine, you must execute 'showmount -e' to refresh the exporting list. The 
second thing is that the OS/2 machine uses the user and group id you have set 
with the configuration program (look in config.sys). This user id is probably 
not 0, which means root. So, you must set the Digital Unix machine to allow 
non-root mounts. This is done with the nfssetup script.

This is what I came to think of.

I am using IBM's TCP/IP package which is not using PC-NFS, but uses the 
ordinary NFS. But you still need to allow for non-root mounts, though.

Good luck.


