x-mailer: Mozilla 2.01 (X11; I; OSF1 V4.0 alpha)

Byron K Guernsey wrote:
> 
> I wrote a program which spawns threads for TCP connections and the parent
> immediately does a pthread_detach() to allow the threads to die without having
> to babysit.
> 
> It appears to work fine.  However, I used the dbx debugger to attach to the
> process and do a tlist to see what threads were running when no threads should
> be running.  It listed 3 threads running, the parent which was in an accept and
> two children who logged as exited correctly in the log file.  The 2 threads
> had already called pthread_exit() but were waiting in some routine named
>  ???_rpc_msg.  I'm positive that I am detaching from them after I create them,
> and if I understood correctly they should be able to exit and cleanup after
> that is done?  Why then does the debugger show these lying in wait?
> 
> Byron
> 

The kernel threads were CACHED by the thread library for re-use -- it's
"relatively expensive" to create a new kernel thread, so they're kept around.
Since dbx is showing you only the kernel threads (dbx knows nothing about user
thread context -- you have to switch to ladebug for that), you still see them
even though they're gone. The message receive call is just a Mach primitive
that happens to be the mechanism used to block the kernel thread until it's
needed again.

