jester@expert.cc.purdue.edu (Jester) writes:

>In setting up the chriscj account, I had accidently left the + off of a
>NIS mapped login.
>chriscj:::::/nfs/h8r2/users/chriscj:/bin/ksh

>One would think that the default UID/GID pair would default to
>65534:65534 which is the Digital default to nobody, and deny the
>login, but it doesn't appear to be the case.

Well a missing value would be 0, although I think several UNIXes refuse
something like 'toor:::::/:' as a minimal passwd entry, it needs to
be 'toor::0:0::/:' for some. The concept of the passwd file is almost
as old as UNIX, from the 70's while UID 65534 was introduced by SUN for
use by NFS/RPC in the mid 80's. Handling UID's is a much lower level
than RPC, which is a user protocol, so it would be unlikely that you want
your kernel to have any knowledge of a special case for UID 65534.

Rob
